  "explains": "findOrder in src/services/orders.ts falls back to the first order. Three files import the service module. src/controllers/orders.ts imports findOrder and uses it in getOrder, so getOrder is a caller; src/controllers/admin.ts imports only countOrders and src/controllers/search.ts only findOrders, so both are excluded by named-export narrowing with their import line and the declaration of the changed function as evidence. src/routes/orders.ts imports getOrder with a named import and wires it in router.get(\"/:id\", requireAuth, getOrder), so the route is GET /api/v1/orders/{id}: the router is the default export of its file, apiRouter mounts it under API_VERSION + \"/orders\" (API_VERSION is \"/v1\" in src/config.ts) and app.use(API_PREFIX, apiRouter) mounts apiRouter under \"/api\". The other handlers of that router do not use findOrder: GET /api/v1/orders uses listOrders (which calls findOrders), the lines routes use findLines and saveLine. The unit test imports findOrder and is a unit test, not a caller. The client builds orderUrl(orderId) as /api/v1/orders/${orderId}, a parameter segment that fits {id}; fetchOrder calls apiGet(orderUrl(orderId)), so the http edge goes to the route and OrderScreen, mounted at /orders/:orderId, is reached. OrdersScreen imports only fetchOrders and is excluded.",
